{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#2955","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#2955","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#2955\",\"parent_revision\":\"26b642aa2176d38bd89067d931c3046930107b48\",\"root_revision\":\"38eb8f829178a97f9b26527703a6bb39c26709afd648b029350d3ceeb06d6fa5\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P2\\ntype: Chore\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-17\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nRun a second, continuous thread across this and future sessions: a working register of every concern, workaround, gotcha, and correction that surfaces from using the development-harness (dh) plugin. A month of accumulated friction with it has gone unrecorded \u2014 capture it in the moment, not from end-of-session memory.\\n\\n## What triggers an entry\\n\\nLog the instant one of these four things happens, mid-task:\\n\\n- **Concern** \u2014 dh behavior that reads as risky, fragile, or wrong, even if it doesn't block the current task.\\n- **Workaround** \u2014 routed around a dh limitation to get the primary task done.\\n- **Gotcha** \u2014 dh did something surprising or non-obvious that cost time to diagnose.\\n- **Correction** \u2014 the user redirected how the agent used dh (a fix to approach, not to what was built).\\n\\n## Entry format\\n\\nAppend via `backlog_groom(item, section=\\\"Working Register\\\", append=True, content=...)`, one call per occurrence:\\n\\n```\\n[category] <one-line summary>\\nWhere: <command / tool / skill invoked>\\nWhat happened: <observed behavior>\\nWhy it matters: <impact or risk>\\n```\\n\\n## End of session\\n\\nCount the session's new entries. At 3+, ask the user whether to spin any into their own backlog items now (`/dh:work-backlog-item create`) or leave them batched for a later grooming pass. Leave this register item open regardless \u2014 it is cumulative, not a single-session task, and closes only when the user decides the log itself should be groomed into standalone items and retired.\\n\\nThis item is BEHAVIORAL/PROCESS: the procedural description above is the requirement specification for the agent's ongoing logging behavior, not implementation instructions to strip.\\n\\n## Working Register\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-17T23:43:40.844852Z</sub>\\n\\n[concern] `backlog_list(from_github=true)` on a full duplicate-check triggers a full-repo sync, not a scoped query\\nWhere: `mcp__plugin_dh_backlog__backlog_list` with `from_github=true` during #2953 grooming/register setup duplicate-check\\nWhat happened: Called to force-refresh the local cache before a title-substring duplicate check. The backend reported 387 open items and the call ran 5+ minutes without completing, getting moved to a background MCP task (`kkra0czcb`) by the harness. No progress/streaming signal during the wait.\\nWhy it matters: There's no apparent way to scope a \\\"refresh\\\" to just the items relevant to a search \u2014 any `from_github=true` call appears to pull the entire backend, making a routine duplicate-check before item creation expensive and slow if you actually need fresh data. In practice this forces reliance on the (possibly stale/incomplete) local cache for time-sensitive duplicate checks, which risks false negatives (missing a real duplicate) on a repo with 387 open items and only ~16 cached.\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-17T23:48:58.000709Z</sub>\\n\\n[concern] `backlog_groom` reports the identical \\\"29 pending mutation(s)\\\" count across consecutive, unrelated writes\\nWhere: `mcp__plugin_dh_backlog__backlog_groom` on #2953 \u2014 two separate calls (RT-ICA section write, then Working Register append) each returned \\\"Reconciled #2953: 1 provider patch(es), 29 pending mutation(s), 0 failure(s).\\\"\\nWhat happened: The pending-mutation count did not change between two consecutive successful writes to two different items (#2953 and #2955), despite each call reporting 0 failures and a successful reconcile.\\nWhy it matters: If pending mutations were actually being drained, the count should trend toward 0 across writes, or at least vary. An unchanging count after \\\"successful\\\" reconciliation suggests either (a) these are stuck/unprocessable queued mutations that reconcile is silently failing to clear, or (b) the reported number isn't what it appears to be (e.g. a static/global count unrelated to this item). Either way it's opaque \u2014 there's no tool surfaced yet to inspect what the 29 pending mutations actually are.\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-17T23:50:10.546865Z</sub>\\n\\n[gotcha] Groom's Wave 0 (technical-researcher) and the discovery gate's investigation cover overlapping ground with no dedup\\nWhere: `/dh:work-backlog-item work #2953` \u2192 routing ran the groom workflow's `analyze.md` discovery gate (spawned `dh:codebase-analyzer` to investigate PyGithub HTTP transport, vcrpy/responses feasibility, Protocol shim inventory) before reaching `swarm.md`'s Wave 0, which independently spawns `technical-researcher` (api-state + ecosystem-research + impact-measurement + codebase-auditor) for the same item's same technology/concern.\\nWhat happened: By the time swarm.md's Wave 0 was reached, the discovery-gate investigation had already produced answers to essentially the same questions Wave 0's 4-angle research would ask (PyGithub API state, vcrpy/responses ecosystem feasibility, codebase audit of github_contents.py). Nothing in the workflow docs flags this overlap or tells the orchestrator to reuse the discovery artifact instead of re-running Wave 0.\\nWhy it matters: Without noticing and manually skipping/merging it, this item's grooming would spawn a second full multi-angle research pass over nearly identical ground the discovery gate just covered \u2014 doubling agent cost and wall-clock time for redundant findings. The workflow has no explicit \\\"check for existing discovery artifact before running Wave 0\\\" step the way analyze.md's discovery gate checks for an existing feature-context artifact before invoking discovery.\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-17T23:51:10.800261Z</sub>\\n\\n[gotcha] `backlog_list(from_github=true)` full-repo cache sync crashed with a filesystem tmp-file race\\nWhere: `mcp__plugin_dh_backlog__backlog_list(search='title:\\\"working register\\\"', include_closed=true, from_github=true)`, backgrounded as MCP task `kkra0czcb` (see prior \\\"full-repo sync\\\" concern entry above \u2014 this is that same call's terminal outcome)\\nWhat happened: After running 10+ minutes, the task failed with `Error calling tool 'backlog_list': [Errno 2] No such file or directory: '/Users/jamienelson/.dh/projects/-Users-jamienelson-repos-claude_skills/github-cache/items/issues/.1175.yaml.vq1wimj_.tmp.yaml'` \u2014 a temp file used for an atomic write to the local YAML cache was missing when something tried to finalize/rename it.\\nWhy it matters: A full-cache refresh (`from_github=true`) is not just slow (see prior entry) \u2014 it can fail outright partway through a 387-item sync with a low-level tmp-file race, leaving the local cache in an unknown partial state with no indication of how many of the 387 items were actually written before the crash. There's no visible retry/resume \u2014 a caller has to notice the failure and decide whether to re-run the full sync from scratch. This makes `from_github=true` risky to rely on for anything time-sensitive (e.g. duplicate detection before creating an item).\\n\\n[concern] Pending-mutation count in `backlog_groom` responses did move (29 \u2192 27) on a later write \u2014 revises the \\\"stuck\\\" read in the earlier entry\\nWhere: Same #2953/#2955 `backlog_groom` calls as the earlier \\\"identical 29 pending mutation(s)\\\" entry\\nWhat happened: A subsequent `backlog_groom` write (Working Register append, immediately after two prior calls both showing 29) returned \\\"27 pending mutation(s)\\\" instead of 29 \u2014 so the queue is draining, just very slowly (2 of 29 cleared across 3+ writes over several minutes), not fully stuck as the earlier entry speculated.\\nWhy it matters: Correcting the record rather than silently editing the earlier entry. The underlying concern still stands \u2014 27+ queued mutations with no visibility into what they are or why they drain so slowly \u2014 but \\\"stuck\\\" was an overreach from only 2 data points; \\\"draining very slowly\\\" is the more accurate read.\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-18T00:12:13.634568Z</sub>\\n\\n[concern] SEVERE \u2014 `backlog_groom` writes report success but are invisible to every read path; section names get mangled/duplicated in the local cache\\nWhere: Full grooming swarm on #2953 \u2014 6 agents (impact-analyst, fact-checker, classifier, rtica-assessor, alignment-analyst, groomer) plus my own orchestrator writes (Research, RT-ICA snapshot), ~15+ `backlog_groom` calls total, every one returning `\\\"groomed_updated\\\": true` with no errors.\\nWhat happened: After the full swarm, `backlog_view(selector=\\\"#2953\\\")` (both MCP, `summary=true` and `summary=false`, and the CLI `backlog view`) still shows only the original 4 sections (Story, Description, Acceptance Criteria [unchanged placeholder text], Context) \u2014 none of RT-ICA, Impact Radius, Fact-Check, Issue Classification, Design Intent Alignment, Research, or the 9 groomer subsections appear, and `status:needs-grooming` label is unchanged. Cross-checked against the real GitHub issue directly (`gh api repos/.../issues/2953`): body is 1869 chars, `updated_at` 2026-08-18T00:08:10Z \u2014 predates the grooming swarm entirely; the live issue never received any of this session's writes.\\n\\nHowever, the content is NOT lost: the local cache file `~/.dh/projects/-Users-jamienelson-repos-claude_skills/github-cache/items/issues/2953.yaml` (298 lines, correct project slug \u2014 confirmed distinct from an unrelated hyphenated `claude-skills` slug directory seen elsewhere) contains all of it, but under **34 top-level section keys** where only ~10-12 should exist. Concretely:\\n- Duplication: `Files` AND `unknown__files` both exist; same pattern for `Resources`/`unknown__resources`, `Reproducibility`/`unknown__reproducibility`, `Impact`/`unknown__impact`, `Impact Radius`/`unknown__impact_radius`, `Dependencies`/`unknown__dependencies`, `Priority`/`unknown__priority`, `Benefits`/`unknown__benefits`, `Research`/`unknown__research`, `Design Intent Alignment`/`unknown__design_intent_alignment`, `Acceptance Criteria`/`unknown__acceptance_criteria`, `Expected Behavior`/`unknown__expected_behavior`.\\n- Case/format mangling with NO canonical counterpart at all: `RT-ICA` was written and stored only as `rt_ica`; `Fact-Check` only as `fact_check`; `Issue Classification` only as `issue_classification` \u2014 none of these three required sections exist under their canonical Title-Case/hyphenated names anywhere in the file.\\n- The fact-checker's per-claim writes fragmented into separate top-level sections instead of one `Fact-Check` section: `unknown__claim_1:_\\\"...\\\"`, `unknown__claim_3:_\\\"...\\\"`, `unknown__claim_4:_\\\"...\\\"`, `unknown__claim_5:_\\\"...\\\"` (claim 2 unaccounted for) \u2014 each with the FULL claim text embedded as part of the YAML key itself.\\n- Bizarrely, even the original, untouched `Story` and `Context` sections got duplicated as `unknown__story` / `unknown__context` despite no agent writing to those sections this session.\\nWhy it matters: This is a correctness bug in `backlog_groom`'s section-name resolution, not a slow-sync issue \u2014 even syntactically correct, exactly-matching canonical section names (`Files`, `Impact`, `Research`, etc.) are invisible to `backlog_view` while sitting in the local cache. If I had proceeded to `finalize.md`'s `mark_groomed=True` write per the groom workflow, the item would have been marked `status:groomed` on the real, shared GitHub issue while its actual content remained empty/unreadable to every consumer, AND any subsequent write or sync risks pushing this 34-key duplicated/malformed structure to the live issue body, corrupting a real repository artifact. Held the primary task's finalize step rather than proceeding, and am reporting this to the user directly instead of forcing a sync.\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-18T00:25:34.341669Z</sub>\\n\\n[correction] Orchestrator noticed workflow inconsistencies in the moment but failed to surface them until the user pressed \u2014 the exact failure mode this register exists to prevent\\nWhere: `/dh:work-backlog-item work #2953`, groom workflow (`swarm.md` + `finalize.md`)\\nWhat happened: Two distinct issues were noticed internally by the orchestrator during execution but not logged or reported until the user explicitly questioned the process several turns later:\\n(1) The groomer dispatch prompt transcribed other agents' section content (RT-ICA verdict, Fact-Check findings, Impact Radius summary) directly into the prompt instead of pointing the agent at `backlog_view(selector=...)` and letting it read independently \u2014 in tension with this repo's own \\\"pass file paths, let agents read; never transcribe file contents into prompts\\\" convention. Traced to `swarm.md`'s \\\"Groomer prompt\\\" section, which itself documents \\\"Input to groomer: item title, description, ..., RT-ICA assessment, Fact-Check verdicts, Issue Classification...\\\" as content to pass, not a pointer to pass \u2014 a portable defect in the dh plugin's own workflow file, not specific to this repo.\\n(2) `finalize.md`'s RT-ICA Final Pass (\\\"The orchestrator \u2014 not a subagent \u2014 executes this,\\\" with mandatory tool-cited self-resolution of every DERIVABLE/MISSING condition) structurally overlaps with `swarm.md` Wave 2's `rtica-assessor` subagent, which already writes the RT-ICA section and returns a verdict. Two steps in the same workflow both claim ownership of producing the same output, with `finalize.md` implying the swarm's subagent-produced version isn't sufficient on its own. This was noticed internally right before proceeding to the next step, and then not surfaced or logged \u2014 it was about to be silently skipped/absorbed rather than flagged as the inconsistency it is.\\nWhy it matters: The working register was set up specifically to capture friction \\\"in the moment, not from end-of-session memory.\\\" Both of these were caught in the moment internally and then dropped rather than surfaced \u2014 meaning the register's core purpose (catching things live) was failing exactly when it mattered, and only the user's direct pushback recovered them. This is a correction to the orchestrator's own behavior, not to dh: notice-and-drop is not the same as notice-and-log; every live observation needs to actually reach either the register or the user before moving to the next step, not get displaced by task momentum.\\n</div>\\n\\n## End Of Session\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nCount the session's new entries. At 3+, ask the user whether to spin any into their own backlog items now (`/dh:work-backlog-item create`) or leave them batched for a later grooming pass. Leave this register item open regardless \u2014 it is cumulative, not a single-session task, and closes only when the user decides the log itself should be groomed into standalone items and retired.\\n\\nThis item is BEHAVIORAL/PROCESS: the procedural description above is the requirement specification for the agent's ongoing logging behavior, not implementation instructions to strip.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **maintainer of the project infrastructure**, I want to **dh plugin working register: ongoing log of concerns, workarounds, gotchas, and corrections** so that **the project infrastructure stays healthy**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## What Triggers An Entry\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nLog the instant one of these four things happens, mid-task:\\n\\n- **Concern** \u2014 dh behavior that reads as risky, fragile, or wrong, even if it doesn't block the current task.\\n- **Workaround** \u2014 routed around a dh limitation to get the primary task done.\\n- **Gotcha** \u2014 dh did something surprising or non-obvious that cost time to diagnose.\\n- **Correction** \u2014 the user redirected how the agent used dh (a fix to approach, not to what was built).\\n</div>\\n\\n## Entry Format\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAppend via `backlog_groom(item, section=\\\"Working Register\\\", append=True, content=...)`, one call per occurrence:\\n\\n```\\n[category] <one-line summary>\\nWhere: <command / tool / skill invoked>\\nWhat happened: <observed behavior>\\nWhy it matters: <impact or risk>\\n```\\n</div>\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Work matches description\\n- [ ] Plan or implementation complete\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Session observation \u2014 direct user instruction to run a continuous, cross-session working register alongside primary task work\\n- **Priority**: P2\\n- **Added**: 2026-08-17\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## Working Register\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-17T23:43:40.844852Z</sub>\\n\\n[concern] `backlog_list(from_github=true)` on a full duplicate-check triggers a full-repo sync, not a scoped query\\nWhere: `mcp__plugin_dh_backlog__backlog_list` with `from_github=true` during #2953 grooming/register setup duplicate-check\\nWhat happened: Called to force-refresh the local cache before a title-substring duplicate check. The backend reported 387 open items and the call ran 5+ minutes without completing, getting moved to a background MCP task (`kkra0czcb`) by the harness. No progress/streaming signal during the wait.\\nWhy it matters: There's no apparent way to scope a \\\"refresh\\\" to just the items relevant to a search \u2014 any `from_github=true` call appears to pull the entire backend, making a routine duplicate-check before item creation expensive and slow if you actually need fresh data. In practice this forces reliance on the (possibly stale/incomplete) local cache for time-sensitive duplicate checks, which risks false negatives (missing a real duplicate) on a repo with 387 open items and only ~16 cached.\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"4f60a8c87ceb5088a75f11e16d71932efed77981f7557cb56f7ee2ad262dd0ca\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABPTV_4w\"}"}